WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:03.689
Film Courage: You mentioned briefly that finding
production funds for an independent film is

00:00:03.689 --> 00:00:04.689
harder these day.

00:00:04.689 --> 00:00:05.860
Hasn’t it always been difficult?

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:08.130
Houston Howard: It absolutely has always been
difficult.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:10.269
It’s never been easy.

00:00:10.269 --> 00:00:17.289
But what’s happened is it’s an already
difficult task has become more difficult because

00:00:17.289 --> 00:00:22.779
to really engage investors and business men
and business women.

00:00:22.779 --> 00:00:27.231
You actually have to make the whole venture
make business sense.

00:00:27.231 --> 00:00:35.100
Now there are always going to be some investors
that have discretionary disposable income

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:41.190
that can just believe in your project and
dump money into it whether or not they get

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:42.670
a return or not.

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:47.440
There’s going to be that marginal group
that you know “Hey, you put my granddaughter

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:52.480
in your movie then I’ll put $100,000 in,
sure!”

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.680
Or they just believe in the purpose of your
project and they’ll dump it in.

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:05.620
That is a rarity and what we have to do is
approach the business of filmmaking from a

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:13.090
perspective of an investor and learn what
motivates an investor, learn the value propositions

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:21.620
behind the business model so that they can
feel comfortable with giving you their money

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:28.840
that they’ve worked very hard for and once
they give you your money, you as a filmmaker

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:33.940
have…here I’ll give you a law school term
is called a fiduciary responsibility, you

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:38.880
have a fiduciary responsibility which is a
legal and ethical responsibility to be able

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:43.850
to take care of that money take care of the
investment and do everything that you can

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:46.430
to get a return on that investment.

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:52.520
And so I never take anybody that gives me
money lightly at all.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:57.700
It’s one of the highest compliments and
one of the biggest responsibilities that you

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.040
have as a filmmaker.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:07.280
Now in order to get that you have to have
the whole thing make business sense, so you

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:14.160
have to imagine what motivates an investor.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.670
It’s probably not your three-act structure.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:23.320
It’s probably not your really amazing b-story
plot, it’s not your interesting sub-plot,

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:30.410
it’s not your plot twists and your act 3,
it’s how do you get them a return on investment.

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:33.100
It’s the amount of ROI.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:38.290
It’s how long is this going to be in the
marketplace, is this a short term investment

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:44.160
or is it a long-term investment, it’s how
big is the market that you’re trying to

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:45.160
attack?

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:48.100
Is it very small or very large?

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:52.470
There’s these things that we’re trying
to figure out.

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:55.470
Filmmakers hate this stuff because they’re
creatives, right?

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:59.420
That’s why directors and writers need good
producers because producers can think this

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:00.420
way.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:09.560
But from my perspective is if a creator, if
the writers and directors, if you can be thinking

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:19.560
this way from the beginning and approach the
creative decisions that you make in your story

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:28.140
in such a way that it optimizes your IP [intellectual
property], your script, your project for larger

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:35.730
investment and larger revenue potential, all
of the sudden that’s the best because you’re

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:40.120
not just leaving it to the “bean counters”
to figure out, you’re not leaving it to

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:42.180
a producer to sell the investment.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:45.300
The creative is optimized for the investment.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:51.709
For example, going back to the principles
of George Lucas.

00:03:51.709 --> 00:03:56.420
Darth Vader…the scene in THE EMPIRE STRIKES
BACK where Darth Vader is talking to the bounty

00:03:56.420 --> 00:04:01.959
hunters, could have been between Darth Vader
and Boba Fett, there didn’t have to be any

00:04:01.959 --> 00:04:03.330
other characters there.

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:07.099
Just Darth Vader saying Bob Fett go catch
Hans Solo.

00:04:07.099 --> 00:04:13.490
But Lucas made the creative decision in the
story to have Boba Fett and eight other bounty

00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:16.680
hunters there in that scene.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:24.249
What that did, that creative decision then
spurred a whole other product that was part

00:04:24.249 --> 00:04:28.840
of the business plan which was this anthology
of short stories that existed in the marketplace

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:35.360
that engaged the fan base and generate revenue,
that all was premised and created and birthed

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:40.419
out of the creative decision of the writer
and director to have multiple characters in

00:04:40.419 --> 00:04:42.210
the scene.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:48.270
So a lot of times there’s been this divide,
this thinking that there’s a divide between

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.460
the creative and the business.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:05:00.210
But if the creative can understand the business
and create in a way that optimizes the project

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:06.229
for the business, then there’s so much opportunity
out there because if it’s not built right

00:05:06.229 --> 00:05:12.770
creatively then that will maximize or limit
the type of business opportunities that you

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:14.889
have down the road.

00:05:14.889 --> 00:05:22.759
So if you can just build the project, present
it right to an investor and it makes business

00:05:22.759 --> 00:05:31.089
sense…I was talking to an investor in Canada
recently who…we were part of a project and

00:05:31.089 --> 00:05:32.089
I call him.

00:05:32.089 --> 00:05:35.809
He’s this big-time angel investor and has
ten thousand angel investors underneath him

00:05:35.809 --> 00:05:37.600
and his network of angel investors.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:39.729
This guy’s a big guy.

00:05:39.729 --> 00:05:44.020
And I got on the phone with him…he said
“Hey, Houston.

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:47.430
I’m going to give you 10 minutes.”

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:48.430
And I said “Okay.”

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:52.930
And he said “I charge $10,000 an hour for
my business consultation.

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:59.680
I don’t speak anywhere for less than $25,000
per event and it’s not fair to all those

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:03.319
people that pay all that money that I give
you a bunch of free time.”

00:06:03.319 --> 00:06:08.379
But because we had a little bit of a relationship
because we spoke at this similar event a few

00:06:08.379 --> 00:06:10.699
year’s prior, he said “I’ll give you
10 minutes free.”

00:06:10.699 --> 00:06:11.699
And I said “Okay.”

00:06:11.699 --> 00:06:15.039
This was a straight business guy.

00:06:15.039 --> 00:06:20.460
I said “Well I want to talk to you about
this film project that we have going on.”

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:25.080
And he stopped me 90 second in and he said
“Listen, out of my 10,000 angel investors,

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:31.710
I could probably only think of 4 who would
even consider investing into entertainment

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:33.119
at all.”

00:06:33.119 --> 00:06:36.819
He said “It’s just to risky.

00:06:36.819 --> 00:06:40.039
If you get a return you’re not going to
get a return for about a year and a half after

00:06:40.039 --> 00:06:41.039
it’s release.

00:06:41.039 --> 00:06:45.650
It’s a difficult market and it’s a difficult
investment to make.

00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:50.379
But maybe there is four and if everything
goes well you may be able to get $100,000

00:06:50.379 --> 00:06:54.569
or $150,000 dollars out of each of them.

00:06:54.569 --> 00:07:01.550
So we’re looking at $200,000 or $250,000
investment if all goes really super well.

00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:07.550
And I said “Well, I appreciate that but
really what we want to do is this really isn’t

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:10.389
going to be a film project, this is going
to be what we call a super story.

00:07:10.389 --> 00:07:14.759
It’s going to be this large scale transmedia
project that’s going to have all of these

00:07:14.759 --> 00:07:18.050
different things as a part of it.”

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:24.000
And I sat there and talked to him for 90 minutes
and I started talking about the different

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:30.069
revenue opportunities, the different markets
that we’re going to touch, the shelf life

00:07:30.069 --> 00:07:33.580
of the project, how we’re going to build
the pre-awareness while the movie is going

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:39.529
to be produced, how we’re going to shift
some things in the advertising budget to more

00:07:39.529 --> 00:07:45.029
content story stuff that we can release simultaneous
to the film so we can extent the experience.

00:07:45.029 --> 00:07:48.060
And then we going to release other things
after the film that’s going to bridge the

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:55.629
gap to the next film and he said to me “In
30 years of investment, no one’s ever approached

00:07:55.629 --> 00:08:01.719
me with an entertainment pitch that has sounded
like this.

00:08:01.719 --> 00:08:07.499
This sounds like a franchisable business more
so than it does a movie.

00:08:07.499 --> 00:08:09.939
Based on this, I think we can do two rounds
of financing.

00:08:09.939 --> 00:08:14.830
I think we could do a 75 million dollar round
of financing and a second round at 50 million

00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:15.830
dollars.

00:08:15.830 --> 00:08:23.129
I could probably give you access to 4,000
of my angels because now it makes business

00:08:23.129 --> 00:08:24.599
sense.”

00:08:24.599 --> 00:08:28.509
And I didn’t even get into to talking to
him about the creative.

00:08:28.509 --> 00:08:32.940
This guys doesn’t even care about my characters,
he doesn’t care about the story, he cares

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:35.479
about the model of it.

00:08:35.479 --> 00:08:41.940
But the creative decisions were the things
that created those opportunities for those

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:43.229
other products and other things.

00:08:43.229 --> 00:08:47.000
So it works together so you can never really
separate it.

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:53.160
And so that just shows you and like the example
of the guy going after the $10,000 investment,

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:58.339
getting a $231,000 investment, that’s the
other end of the spectrum but the result is

00:08:58.339 --> 00:08:59.339
very similar.

00:08:59.339 --> 00:09:02.589
One thing doesn’t make business sense but
when you can now create a project that makes

00:09:02.589 --> 00:09:10.350
business sense now everything makes much more
sense and what it does it hedges the investment,

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:16.779
it makes the investment more secure because
all investors are risk-averse, they don’t

00:09:16.779 --> 00:09:18.730
just want to throw their money away.

00:09:18.730 --> 00:09:24.380
And so if you can make them more comfortable
with the fact that the investment is secure,

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:28.800
now they say “I’m part of this for the
long term, there’s an opportunity here,

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:30.220
here, here and here.

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:32.769
Now it just makes a lot more sense for them.

00:09:32.769 --> 00:09:36.810
It’s still difficult, don’t get me wrong,
it’s still difficult but you just increase

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:40.740
the chances for success and also separate
yourself in the marketplace.

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:48.310
And so Tim Kring (the guy who created Heroes),
he just got a 50 million dollar investment

00:09:48.310 --> 00:09:52.220
into a transmedia company that he started
with another guy here in town called Zach

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:58.199
Kasten and they created a whole transmedia
company and they went to an oil man in Texas

00:09:58.199 --> 00:10:03.471
and the oil man said “I’ve never invested
in entertainment, ever in my whole life, but

00:10:03.471 --> 00:10:08.250
when they approached me with this trans media
model it made business sense.

00:10:08.250 --> 00:10:11.250
And so he dumped 50 million dollars into their
venture.

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:20.709
So what it does is it gives you access to
a different type of investment and more investors

00:10:20.709 --> 00:10:26.550
when you can really understand how markets
work, how we can get more audience, how we

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:32.650
can get different types of audiences, how
we can diversify income, etc.

00:10:32.650 --> 00:10:34.260
All this sounds very uncreative, right?

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:38.450
I mean you know I can see like screenwriters
watching this video and probably cringing

00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:39.639
right now.

00:10:39.639 --> 00:10:45.970
But what was really interesting is none of
these other opportunities of the tie-ins or

00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:52.680
the spin-offs or the other products that you
can surround your project with, those won’t

00:10:52.680 --> 00:11:01.330
be possible or at least possible in a good
way if you don’t create the story to be

00:11:01.330 --> 00:11:03.450
able to optimize it from that.

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:05.060
Go back to the Lucas example.

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:09.970
He made the creative decisions to have 8 characters
in a scene rather than 2.

00:11:09.970 --> 00:11:14.470
That then fueled hundreds of thousands of
dollars worth of revenue and engagement opportunity

00:11:14.470 --> 00:11:16.350
for the fanbase.

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:18.020
So the story drives the business.

